<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.10.6 Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01034</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.10.6 Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>25116715-383f-4477-867f-3a35f6088c31</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <para>Use the following scenario and graph to answer question 1. </para>
      <para>
        <quote>Functions \(R\) and \(D\) give the height, in feet, of a toy rocket and a drone, \(t\) seconds after they are released.</quote>
      </para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="dda9a192-afb9-4d84-af71-cf27430be6a1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Here are the graphs of \(R\) (for the rocket) and \(D\) (for the drone).</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Two functions graphed on a cooridnate plane.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_13_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Decide whether the following statements are true or false.</para>
            <para> \(R(3)&gt;D(3)\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! True</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.10.4. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is true. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="8d4965ce-4ed6-47c7-84a5-8f386fd24131" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> \(R(0)=D(0)\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! False</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.10.4. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is false. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="6dfc5704-a1ba-4f7b-a75e-ca6124401256" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> \(R(4.5)=D(4.5)\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! True</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.10.4. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is true. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="806539da-ea72-4105-8022-54c376c3d87a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> \(R(0)=D(0)+25\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! True</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.10.4. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is true. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="1d35db9a-6af8-44ae-bec5-d9bb9b0cfad3" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>\(D(7)&gt;R(5)\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! False</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.10.4. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is false. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="0ddaab9d-60de-4e61-a407-65e98a20f548" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>\(A(t)\) is the average high temperature in Aspen, Colorado, \(t\) months after the start of the year. \(M(t)\) is the average high temperature in Minneapolis, Minnesota, \(t\) months after the start of the year. Temperature is measured in degrees Fahrenheit.</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_13_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Which function had the higher average rate of change between the beginning of January and middle of March?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Minneapolis</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.10.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Minneapolis. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="9a273719-f18c-482a-b3ba-fe24ef89a14f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Here are two graphs representing functions \(f\) and \(g\). 
          <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_13_03.svg"/></media></figure><emphasis>Select the three </emphasis>statements that are true about functions \(f\) and \(g\). </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! There are values of \(x\) where \(f(x)=g(x)\), \(f(-1)&lt;g(-1)\), and \(f(-3)&gt;g(4)\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.10.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answers are: There are values of \(x\) where \(f(x)=g(x)\), \(f(-1)&lt;g(-1)\), and \(f(-3)&gt;g(4)\). <g> </g>
      
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="4e794bf6-e260-4a61-a58a-17ac911bdea1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>For questions 4 – 6, use the three graphs below:</para>
            <para>
              <quote>The three graphs represent the progress of three runners in a 400-meter race.
         The solid line represents Runner A. The dotted line represents Runner B. The dashed line represents Runner C.</quote>
            </para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_13_04.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Which runner ran at a constant rate throughout the race?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Runner C</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.10.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Runner C. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="04ab420e-4add-46e5-882c-2d284935828d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which runner stopped running for a while?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Runner A</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.10.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Runner A. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f6688a12-f180-460e-adbe-77007465867b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which runner won the race?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Runner C</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.10.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Runner C. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="6051a616-ba9f-4e8b-9a5f-e1609189532b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Use the graphs below for questions 7 – 8.</para>
            <para>Show A                                             Show B                                   Show C</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_13_05.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Which show had the largest audience for episode 4?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Show B</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.10.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is Show B. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="59eb0e33-4424-4a1b-8eae-2ef7a75ce41c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which show had the largest audience for their first episode?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Show B</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.10.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is Show B. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
